a question the warehouse cannot answer
  query_warehouse({"question": "how many staff work in Ohio"})
  is_error=True  Error executing tool query_warehouse: Refused: Needs a headc

a tool that does not exist
  delete_everything({})
  is_error=True  Unknown tool: delete_everything

a required argument left out
  query_warehouse({})
  is_error=True  Error executing tool query_warehouse: 1 validation error for

an argument of the wrong type
  search_documents({"query": "revenue", "limit": "lots"})
  is_error=True  Error executing tool search_documents: 1 validation error fo

Every one of those came back as a tool error, including two that are not the
model's fault at all: a tool that does not exist, and arguments that do not
type-check. This SDK's default is to hand almost everything to the model.

A protocol error looks different, and you have to go off the tool path to
provoke one:

  {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 9, "error": {"code": -32601, "message": "Method not
  no 'result', no content, code -32601

The distinction is not cosmetic. It decides who is expected to act.

A tool error is a result. It travels in `content`, a model reads it, and the
run continues. Clarity's warehouse refusal is a paragraph telling the model
what the data does hold — Chapter 16 measured terse errors leading to a right
call 3 times in 24, and instructive ones 21.

A protocol error is a bug. No content, no model, nothing downstream that can
recover. It should page somebody.

Which means the line is not where you would put it. 'Unknown tool' is a
client bug being reported to a model as advice, and a validation failure is a
schema disagreement being handed to the one participant who cannot fix it.
If you want your outages to reach you rather than politely confuse a model,
draw that boundary yourself — the protocol will not draw it for you.
